From 8c85ca1a08700e3e6453fc4f5fce7cfe77399487 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Eric <Atoramos@Gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 18 Jul 2026 13:20:35 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] fix(arcade): coordinator server-role sockets no longer starve
 their own read deadline

0035 fixed a slow inline handler starving the CLIENT-role (pingPong=false)
read deadline by having the writer send unsolicited pongs. It never touched
the SERVER-role (pingPong=true) branch, because at the time
com.DefaultCallTimeout (10s) bounded every RPC so no handler could run long
enough to matter either direction. 0042 later gave StartGame its own 60s
budget, which reopened the identical starvation on the coordinator's OWN
sockets (both user-facing and worker-facing are pingPong=true): HandleStartGame
runs inline in the read pump and blocks it for up to 60s, during which the
pump never calls ReadMessage() and so never sees an incoming pong no matter
how promptly the peer answers - the deadline expires and the peer's own
healthy reply arrives too late to matter, and the room dies right after it
finishes loading.

Unlike 0035's case, an unsolicited pong FROM the peer can't fix this - the
stuck reader is what would have to process it, and it's exactly what's
blocked. Fix instead extends the read deadline directly from the writer
goroutine (which is never blocked by the reader) every ping tick, regardless
of whether a reply was received. A genuinely dead peer is still caught via
the ping WRITE failing once the TCP connection is actually gone.
---
 pkg/network/websocket/websocket.go | 13 +++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+)

diff --git a/pkg/network/websocket/websocket.go b/pkg/network/websocket/websocket.go
index 457c6ff..e6ef733 100644
--- a/pkg/network/websocket/websocket.go
+++ b/pkg/network/websocket/websocket.go
@@ -202,6 +202,19 @@ func (c *Connection) writer() {
 				if err := c.conn.write(websocket.PingMessage, nil); err != nil {
 					return
 				}
+				// Extend OUR OWN read deadline directly, rather than relying on the peer's pong
+				// reply being processed by reader()'s ReadMessage loop. That loop can be blocked for
+				// the WHOLE DURATION of a long inline handler (HandleStartGame blocks up to
+				// coordinator.GameStartTimeout=60s waiting on the worker) — during which it never
+				// calls ReadMessage(), so it never sees ANY pong no matter how promptly the peer
+				// (browser or worker) answers. That starved this socket's OWN pongTime deadline even
+				// though the peer was perfectly healthy — the exact bug 0035 fixed on the CLIENT
+				// (pingPong=false) branch above, reopened here on the SERVER branch once 0042 gave
+				// StartGame a budget bigger than pongTime. See the "OPEN GAP" note in
+				// docker/arcade/patches/README.md (right after 0042) for the full trace.
+				// A genuinely dead peer is still caught: the ping WRITE above fails once the TCP
+				// connection is actually gone — same detection 0035 already relies on.
+				_ = c.conn.SetReadDeadline(time.Now().Add(pongTime))
 			}
 		}
 	} else {
